%[text] # Debugging Simulation Results with an AI Coding Agent
%[text] [![](text:image:6447)](https://matlab.mathworks.com/open/github/v1?repo=simulink/blog&file=2026_11_16_Debugging_with_AI\\matlab.toml)
%[text] I have to admit, in my day-to-day work, I spend a lot more time debugging Simulink models that produce unexpected results than creating new ones. Since I have access to AI coding agents, I have had a lot of success speeding up debugging sessions and getting deeper insights than I could have dreamed of before.
%[text] Today I decided to share one example of this kind of debugging.
%[text] ## The problem
%[text] A colleague reached out saying they were trying to apply the technique I described in [Getting the most out of Rapid Accelerator mode](https://blogs.mathworks.com/simulink/2023/10/05/getting-the-most-out-of-rapid-accelerator-mode-version-r2023b/), but they were getting unexpected results. The model was made of more than 100,000 blocks from many different blocksets and used thousands of parameters. 
%[text] We offer many tools, diagnostics, and documentation pages to understand and debug this type of issue. Even with all those resources, running into an issue like this one can be intimidating.
%[text] For this blog post, I reproduced the original problem in a smaller project, using a slightly modified version of the Simscape Driveline example [Series Hybrid Transmission](https://www.mathworks.com/help/sdl/ug/series-hybrid-transmission.html).
%[text] ![](text:image:4be0)
%[text] I stored most of the variables needed by the model in a data dictionary:
%[text] ![](text:image:9b43)
%[text] Then I created a second parameter set with different values for most variables.
%[text] ![](text:image:0a66)
%[text] I simulated the model with both parameter sets in Normal mode and in Rapid Accelerator mode. For the Rapid Accelerator runs, I set `RapidAcceleratorUpToDateCheck` to `off` for maximum performance.
mdl = 'SeriesHybridTransmission';
in = repmat(Simulink.SimulationInput(mdl),4,1);
Simulink.BlockDiagram.buildRapidAcceleratorTarget(mdl); %[output:9a8629b1]
in(1) = in(1).setModelParameter('SimulationMode','Normal');
in(2) = in(2).setModelParameter('SimulationMode','Normal');
in(2) = in(2).loadVariablesFromMATFile('SeriesHybridTransmission_alternate_variables.mat');
in(3) = in(3).setModelParameter('SimulationMode','Rapid',...
                                'RapidAcceleratorUpToDateCheck','off');
in(4) = in(4).loadVariablesFromMATFile('SeriesHybridTransmission_alternate_variables.mat');
in(4) = in(4).setModelParameter('SimulationMode','Rapid',...
                                'RapidAcceleratorUpToDateCheck','off');
out = sim(in,'ShowProgress','off');
figure %[output:953016ee]
hold on %[output:953016ee]
styles = {'-','-','o','o'};
for i=1:4
    plot(out(i).logsout.get('Velocity').Values,styles{i}) %[output:953016ee]
end
legend({'Normal - Dataset 1',... %[output:group:93665e70] %[output:953016ee]
    'Normal - Dataset 2',... %[output:953016ee]
    'Rapid Accelerator - Dataset 1',... %[output:953016ee]
    'Rapid Accelerator - Dataset 2'},"Location","northwest"); %[output:group:93665e70] %[output:953016ee]
%[text] Looking at the results, we can see that the Normal and Rapid Accelerator results match for parameter set 1. The run in Normal mode with parameter set 2 returns expected results, but the run in Rapid Accelerator mode with parameter set 2 gives unexpected results.
%[text] ## Asking for help
%[text] A year ago, I would have started manually digging into that. This time, I executed [shareMATLABSession](https://github.com/matlab/matlab-mcp-server/blob/main/matlab/matlab_mcp_toolbox/mcp/shareMATLABSession.m), launched the AI agent from the same folder as the MATLAB project and asked:
%[text] ***`See repro.m. It shows unexpected results in Rapid Accelerator for case 4. Explain why and how to fix it. Don't change the model, let me review.`***
%[text] To begin, the agent loaded relevant skills from the Simulink Agentic Toolkit:
%[text] ![](text:image:1282)
%[text] The agent ran a few MATLAB commands using the `evaluate_matlab_code` tool, loaded the `matlab-read-documentation` skill to gather more info and quickly responded with:
%[text] ![](text:image:4772)
%[text] To address my request for possible ways to fix the problem, I got this answer:
%[text] ![](text:image:84bc)
%[text] I asked the agent to set the configurability of the vehicle mass parameter to Run-time, as suggested in option 2. I then confirmed that the simulation results matched the expected results.
%[text] ## Learning opportunity
%[text] When I solve a problem using an AI agent, I like to ask how it diagnosed the issue and which tools I could have used to investigate it myself. So I asked:
%[text] ***`Summarize which tools and techniques I could have used to understand and debug that myself manually`***
%[text] I also like to ask the agent to generate a Markdown or HTML report of its findings. Here is a screenshot from the HTML report I got:
%[text] ![](text:image:068d)
%%
%[text] ## Creating a repeatable tool
%[text] While an AI agent can speed up an investigation like this one where I was not sure what to look for and the scope was large, it's not always the most efficient solution. Once a problematic pattern has been identified, it's usually faster to call a function that does the work efficiently in a repeatable manner. To create this function, I asked the AI agent:
%[text] ***`I would like to write a MATLAB function to detect this situation in the future. The function should take the simulationInput object as input and check the existing build_rtp.mat in slprj. The function should return a table with one entry per variable in the simulationInput identified as non-tunable. It should list the variable name, the default and new values, the blockpath of the block(s) where it's used and a reason. For now, include the Simscape configurability reason and keep a placeholder for other future reasons we might discover.`*** 
%[text] Within a few seconds, the function was created. Here is what it returns:
T = findNonTunableOverrides(in(4)) %[output:4be3aafd]
%[text] ## Conclusion
%[text] Simscape parameters need to have their configurability set to Run-time to be tuned in Rapid Accelerator mode and not trigger a rebuild. We have a full [documentation section on that topic](https://www.mathworks.com/help/simscape/run-time-parameters.html).
%[text] ![](text:image:1ac5)
%[text] The agent was especially useful because the problematic parameter was buried among thousands of others. Investigating manually could have taken considerable time: inspecting parameters, comparing them with simulation results, writing inspection code, and stepping through code in the debugger.
%[text] While digging further, I confirmed that the `sim` command issues a warning for this behavior. In the original project, however, it was easy to miss.
%[text] ![](text:image:9d19)
%[text] Once the agent helped me understand the problem, I asked it to create a simple function to detect similar issues before trying to simulate the model.
%[text] ## Now it's your turn
%[text] What kinds of problems are you debugging with the help of AI agents? Let us know in the comments below.

